Even more so, actually, in the long run. And this is the real kind of, the lawless kind of badlands of Japan. The roughest, the most militaristic, the most samurai infested reaches of Honshu, the main island in Japan. And as a result of this, I would say by the end of the 11th century, the Minamoto are starting to outmustle the Taira. And so now when there is violence in these northeastern provinces, it's the Minamoto who are getting to present themselves as the agents of law and order and to cast the Tyra as the outlaws. So in 1027, for instance, there's a Minamoto warlord who topples a Tyra rival by writing to the imperial palace and saying this guy, this Tyra, he is a rat of wolf-like greed who had turned the structure of the court upside down, collecting taxes and tax goods for himself and ignoring imperial orders. And this is symptomatic of a broader trend so that by the 12th century, the Minamoto have clearly established themselves as the dominant power in these eastern provinces that lie to the
将它们与京都隔开的山脉以东。有两个特定的地区是他们的据点。一个是高地地区，叫信浓，位于本州岛中部的内陆。如果你看日本地图，本州岛，信浓基本上就在正中间。它几乎是坚不可摧的，因为地形非常多山，但它被一条叫木曾川的河流切割，这形成了一个切割山脉的峡谷，既易守难攻，又在战略上至关重要，因为它提供了一条通道，让军队……

21
00:14:27.000 --> 00:15:07.000
east of the mountains that separate them from Kyoto. And there are two particular regions that are their strongholds. So one of them is an upland region, called Shinano, absolutely in the interior of central Honshu. So if you look at the map of Japan, the main island of Honshu, Shinano is kind of basically bang in the middle. And it's almost impregnable because the terrain is so mountainous, except that it is scored by this river called Kiso, and this provides a kind of valley that scores the mountains, and it's easily defensible, but it's also strategically vital because it provides a way that army
从信浓的山区高地顺着这个山谷流出，进入平原。这就是木曾谷。所以现在如果你想看那种老式的日本村庄，那种非常风景如画、非常适合旅游的地方，就像日本的科茨沃尔德，这就是你要去的地方。必须说，在11世纪，它一点也不适合旅游，但它在这个故事中将占据重要地位。它确实非常多山。如果你想建立一个与京都抗衡的权力基地，你真正需要的是平原，在那里你可以种植水稻、食物和各种东西。源氏在本州最大的平原建立了他们的权力基地，这就是关东平原。所以字面意思是“关之东”，即……

22
00:15:07.000 --> 00:15:55.000
from the mountainous heights of Shinano can kind of go down this valley and spill out into the plain. This is the Kiso Valley. So this is now where you go if you want to see kind of old-fashioned Japanese villages and kind of, you know, it's very picturesque and very touristy, kind of the cotswolds of Japan. It wasn't remotely touristy back in the 11th century. It has to be said, and it will be featuring strongly in this story. But it is very mountainous. And what you really want, if you're going to set up a rival power base to Kyoto is a plane, you know, where you can grow rice and food and all kinds of things. And the Minamoto establish their power base in the largest plane in Honshu, and this is the plane of Kanto. So literally east of the barrier, so the barrier
将它与京都隔开的山脉。它比关西（京都所在的关之西的平原）更广阔，潜力也大得多。在12世纪，它与关西相比仍然非常不发达，但充满了潜力。在这一时期，它见证了一个叫“江户”的小定居点的建立，它在适当的时候将成为东京，即东方的首都，也就是今天的日本首都。所以，到了12世纪，本州岛的东半部基本上处于源氏的控制之下。他们拥有信浓这个巨大的山区，以及关东平原。这是否意味着他们现在是整个日本的主导力量？

23
00:15:55.000 --> 00:16:47.000
is the mountain separating it from Kyoto. It's broader. It's potentially much richer than Kanzai, the plain west of the barrier on which Kyoto stands. And it is in the 12th century still very undeveloped compared to Kanzai, but it is full of potential. And in this period, it sees the establishment of a small settlement called Edo, which in due course will become Tokyo, the eastern capital, so the capital of Japan today. So, the eastern half of Honshu by the 12th century is essentially under the thumb of the Minamoto. They have Shinano, this great kind of mountainous region, and they have the plain of Kanto. Does this mean, therefore, that they are now the dominant power across the whole of Japan?
多米尼克，这并不意味着，因为尽管平氏基本上放弃了本州岛的东半部给源氏，但他们还有别的事情要做。因为与源氏相比，他们更多地保留了传统日本贵族的视角，认为本州岛的东部地区是落后的、野蛮的，根本不是绅士该去的地方。而岛屿的西半部，特别是关西，因为那里有京都，那个大平原，那才是真正重要的地方。这就是为什么在11和12世纪，他们选择从本州岛的东部撤出，开始将精力集中在那些一直是日本帝国传统核心地带的地方。

24
00:16:47.000 --> 00:17:40.000
Dominic, it does not mean that, because although the Taira have essentially abandoned the eastern half of Honshu to the Minamoto, they do have other fish to fry. Because much more than the Minamoto, they have kind of retained the perspective of the traditional Japanese aristocracy, the sense that the eastern reaches of Honshu are backward, are savage, not the place for a gentleman to be seen at all. And it's the western half of the island. And Kansai, particularly, because that's where Kyoto is, the Great Plain, it's Kansai that really matters. And this is why over the course of the 11th and 12th century, countries, they had opted to withdraw from the eastern reaches of Honshu and start to focus their energies on what have always been the kind of traditional heartlands of the Japanese Empire.
因此，到1150年，他们已经确立了自己作为日本西部明确的主导力量，而且不仅是在陆地上，因为他们还控制着所谓的“濑户内海”。对于那些不熟悉日本地理的人来说，西南部的岛屿，日本群岛的最底端，就像一个岛屿化的德文郡和康沃尔郡。然后还有另一个岛叫四国，就像一个巨大的怀特岛，沿着南部海岸一直延伸。是的，比怀特岛大得多。大得多。所以这两个紧邻本州岛的岛屿，即九州和四国，创造了所谓的濑户内海。濑户内海的意义在于，它保护航运免受否则会席卷而来并构成持续危险的猛烈风暴。因此，这些是日本水域中迄今为止最安全的航道。平氏基本上确立了对这些水域的控制。在1140年代和1150年代，一位才华横溢、雄心勃勃的年轻总督清盛为他们巩固了这些航道。

25
00:17:40.000 --> 00:19:04.000
And so by 1150, they have established themselves as clearly the dominant power in Western Japan and tellingly not just by land, because they also control what is called the inland sea. and for those not familiar with the geography of Japan, the southwestern island, the bottom tip of the Japanese archipelago, it's like a kind of Devon and Cornwall that is an island. And then there's another island called Shikoku, and that is like a massive Isle of White, which runs all the way along the kind of the southern coast. Yeah, much bigger than the Isle of White. Much, much bigger than the Isle of White. And so these two islands that abut Honshu, the main island, so Kushu and Shikuku, they kind of create this, what's called the inland sea. And the point of the inland sea is that it protects shipping from the violent storms that otherwise would kind of sweep down and be a constant danger. And so these are by far the securest shipping lanes anywhere, you know, the waters off Japan. And the Tyra essentially established their control over these waters. and they are particularly secured for them in the 1140s and 1150s by a brilliantly able and ambitious young governor who goes by the name of Kiomori.
清盛在本州南部海岸和四国北部海岸建立了新的港口。他疏浚航道。他使航运路线更加安全。一旦这些航运路线得到保障，它们反过来使平氏能够控制贸易，从而为他们创造了巨大的财富，并使他们能够比陆路更容易地调动军队，因为正如我们所说，日本非常多山。所以通过船只运送军队比走陆路快得多。当然，这也提供了通往京都的便捷通道，因为京都所在的平原……

26
00:19:04.000 --> 00:19:42.000
And Kiomori founds new ports, both along the southern coast of Honshu and the northern coast of Shikoku. He dredges channels. He makes the shipping lanes more secure. and these shipping lanes in turn, once they've been secured, enable the Tyra to control trade, which thereby generates enormous wealth for them, to move troops much more easily than they can by land, because as we said, Japan is very, very mountainous. So taking troops by ships, you know, it's much faster than going by road. And of course, it provides ready access to Kyoto, because the plane on which Kyoto stands
面向濑户内海。如果你控制了京都，那么你当然就控制了皇宫。如果你控制了皇宫，那么你当然就控制了天皇。到目前为止，平氏和源氏之间的权力变得如此巨大，以至于他们中的一些人，无论是平氏还是源氏，都开始考虑一种诱人的可能性，多米尼克，你一直说，这在长期来看总是会发生的。这种可能性是：如果他们直接夺取对天皇的控制权，并把他当作傀儡会怎样？嗯，如果有一群人拿着武器、剑，而另一群人拿着书法工具，我总是押注于那些拿剑的人。

27
00:19:42.000 --> 00:20:32.000
is open to the inland sea. And if you control Kyoto, then of course you control the palace. And if you control the palace, then you control the emperor. And by now, the power of the Tyra and the Minamoto between them is becoming so preponderant that some of them, both Tyra and Minamoto, are starting to contemplate a tantalizing possibility, which, Dominic, you've been saying, you know, it was always something that was going to kick in in the long run. And this possibility is, what if they seize direct control of the emperor and use him as a puppet? Well, if there's a load of people armed with weapons, swords, and there's a lot of people armed with calligraphy kits, my money is always on them, people with the swords.
我发现我是对的，这并不奇怪。你是对的。因为正如我们在上一集中所说，京都的贵族们一直表现得好像历史已经终结，好像你不需要武装自己，好像过着诗歌、求爱和美感的生活永远不会结束。但正如常有的那样，历史永远不会终结。如果你不武装自己，你总是会面临问题。对于京都的朝廷来说，危机点出现在一个非常具体的日子。

28
00:20:32.000 --> 00:21:06.000
I'm not surprised to discover that I was right. You are right. Because all this time, as we said in the previous episode, the nobility in Kyoto have been behaving as though history has come to an end, as though you don't need to arm yourself. as though, you know, living a life of poetry and courtship and beauty and so on, as though that's never going to come to an end. But as always happens, you know, history never comes to an end. And if you don't harm yourself, you are always going to face problems. And for the court in Kyoto, the crisis point comes on a very specific day.
这就是1156年7月20日。这一天，那位在京都担任皇位背后权力者超过30年的人去世了。他的官方头衔是“上皇”，一个叫鸟羽的人。他是上皇，因为他曾是天皇。他年少时即位，20岁时退位并进入寺院。他从寺院里作为皇室无可争议的族长进行统治。所以年轻的天皇继承了他，但上皇鸟羽本质上仍然是掌握权力缰绳的人。这听起来可能……

29
00:21:06.000 --> 00:21:51.000
And this is the 20th of July 1156. And it comes with the death of the man who for more than 30,000, years had served as the power behind the imperial throne in Kyoto. And this was, his official title is his cloistered eminence, a man called Tobar. And he's a cloistered eminence because he had been emperor. He'd become emperor as a boy. He had then abdicated at the age of 20 and gone to a monastery. And he had presided from this monastery as the kind of the undisputed patriarch of the imperial family. So young emperors have succeeded him, but his cloistered eminence, Tober, remains essentially the guy who has the reins of power in his hands. And this may sound
对听众来说很疯狂。我的意思是，如果你是天皇，为什么要退休成为和尚之类的？但这是到12世纪中叶，皇位继承运作了三个世纪的方式。整个做法是日本三大政治王朝中第三个王朝的结晶，我们在关于紫式部的系列节目中提到过，但在那之前我们没提过。这个王朝叫藤原氏。藤原氏没有像平氏和源氏那样去省份建立自己的权力基地。藤原氏留在了京都，他们的行动领域就是宫廷本身。引用伊万·莫里斯的话……

30
00:21:51.000 --> 00:22:36.000
mad to listeners. I mean, why, if you're the emperor, are you kind of retiring and becoming a monk and all this kind of thing? But it's the way that the imperial succession had been operating for basically three centuries by this point, by the mid-12th century. And the whole practice had been the brainchild of the third of the three great political dynasties in Japan, which we haven't mentioned until now, although we did in our series on Lady Murasaki. And this is a dynasty called the Fujiwara. And the Fujiwara had not done what the Tyra and the Minamoto had done, go out into the provinces and build a power base for themselves there. The Fujiwara had remained in Kyoto, and their field of operations had been the court itself. And to quote Ivan Morris,
他在关于紫式部和源氏物语世界（即“光之君”的世界）的精彩著作中写道：藤原氏强加给天皇一种几乎注定要让他处于家族控制之下的生命周期，也就是藤原氏的控制之下。他作为一名稚嫩的青年即位，并立即与一名藤原氏女孩结婚。他们的儿子将被任命为皇太子，当他的父亲通常在30岁左右被迫退位时，他将继位，循环再次开始。显然，只要没有暴力的武士王朝在你脖子上喘气，这对藤原氏来说是有效的。但问题是，到了12世纪中叶，武士不再局限于遥远的省份。他们越过了山脉，

31
00:22:36.000 --> 00:23:21.000
his brilliant book on the world of Lady Murasaki and Genji, the world of the shining prince. The Fujiwara had imposed on the emperor a type of life cycle that was almost bound to keep him under the family's thumb, so the Fujiwara's thumb. He came to the throne as a callow youth and was promptly married to a Fujiwara girl. Their son would be appointed crown prince, and when his father was obliged to abdicate usually at the age of about 30, he would succeed him and the cycle would start again. And obviously, this works for the Fujiwara as long as you don't have violent warrior dynasties breathing down your neck. But the problem is that by the middle of the 12th century, the samurai are no longer confined to distant provinces. They've crossed the mountains,
进入了京都，他们在首都的街道上游荡。结果，藤原氏统治的时代开始走向衰落。因为从现在开始，将是平氏和源氏共同决定皇位继承。随着上皇鸟羽的去世，平氏和源氏的敌对派系支持皇位的竞争候选人。所以此时，并不是平氏支持一个人，源氏支持一个人。

32
00:23:21.000 --> 00:23:55.000
they've entered Kyoto, they are roaming the streets of the capital. And as a result, the age of Fujiwara dominance is starting to go into eclipse. Because, from now on it is going to be the Tyra and the Minamoto, who between them decide the imperial succession. And with the death of his cloistered eminence, Tober rival factions of Tyra and Minamoto backing rival candidates for the throne. So at this point, it's not like the Tyra are backing one guy and the Minamoto are backing one guy.
平氏和源氏混在一起，但这些不同的派系支持皇位的竞争候选人。这预示着危险。问一个愚蠢的问题：如果鸟羽去世了，他不是天皇，为什么会有继承危机？因为鸟羽作为家族族长，在决定谁将继承他方面发挥着关键作用。所以他不是官方的天皇，但他确实拥有真正的权力，因为他的威望、年龄、智慧和老练意味着他的意见受到尊重，甚至藤原氏也必须尊重它。但现在他走了，没有……

33
00:23:55.000 --> 00:24:34.000
Tyra and Minamoto are kind of mixing up, but these different factions are backing rival candidates for the throne. And this spells danger. ask a dim-witted question. Why if Tober has died and he wasn't emperor, why is there a succession crisis? Because Tobar in his role as the patriarch of the family plays a crucial role in deciding who is going to succeed him. So he is not officially emperor, but he does have authentic power because his prestige, his age, his intelligence, his sophistication means that his opinions are respected and even the Fujiwara have to kind of respect it. But now that he is gone, there isn't
任何人来扮演那个角色。这就是为什么这些平氏和源氏的敌对派系能够介入并填补真空。情况演变成了暴力，战斗在一夜之间就结束了。其原因说明了京都的朝臣们变得多么与世隔绝和天真。其中一个平氏-源氏派系受到一位藤原氏权贵的左右，他是一个非常虔诚地信奉孔子教义的人。所以此时在皇都，中国文化仍受到极大的尊重。因为孔子说伏击别人是非常糟糕的行为，你应该按规则行事。你知道，不要破坏游戏规则。他说发动突然袭击是违法的。

34
00:24:34.000 --> 00:25:32.000
anyone to play that role. And so this is why these rival factions of Tyro Minamoto are able to step in and fill the vacuum. And it turns to violence and the fighting is all over in a single night. And the reason for that tells you a lot about how cloistered and naive the courtiers in Kyoto have become. So one of these kind of Tyra Minamoto factions, they are swayed by a Fujiwara Grandi, who is a man very devoted to the teachings of Confucius. So still at this point in the Imperial Capital, Chinese culture is viewed with enormous respect. And so because Confucius says that it's very bad form to ambush people, you should play according to the rules. You know, don't rip up the rules of the game. He says it's against the law to launch a surprise attack.
实际上，武士们全都是关于突然袭击的。这是他的愚蠢。珍珠港事件并非凭空而来。这有着悠久的武士传统。另一个派系，他们根本不在乎孔子。所以他们说，当然我们要发动袭击。于是他们在半夜伏击了对手。他们烧毁了他们的总部，并赢得了决定性的胜利。胜利派系的两位领袖，一个是源氏，一个是平氏，然后，故事是这样说的……

35
00:25:32.000 --> 00:26:05.000
Actually, the samurai are all about surprise attacks. This is idiocy from him. The bombing of Pearl Harbor didn't come from nowhere. This is a long samurai tradition. The other faction, they don't give a toss about Confucius. And so they say, well, of course we're going to launch an attack. So they ambush their opponents in the middle of the night. They burned down their headquarters, and they win a decisive victory. And the two leaders of the victorious faction, one is a Minamoto, one is a Tyra, they then, so the story goes,
他们用被俘对手的鲜血签署了盟约。这有点像第二次三头政治，安东尼、屋大维和雷必达，把手指浸在他们自己亲戚的鲜血中。所以这两位领袖之一，一个叫源义朝的人，据说公开斩首了他自己的父亲。天哪，拜托。我的意思是，这有点过分了。你必须做你必须做的事。而另一个……

36
00:26:05.000 --> 00:26:31.000
they seal their pact with the blood of their captured opponents. So it's a bit like with the second triumvirate, Anthony and Octavian and Leopardus, kind of dipping their fingers in the blood of their own relatives. So one of these two leaders, a guy called Minamoto no Yoshitomo, he is said to have publicly beheaded his own father, Jesus, come on. I mean, that's a bit strong. You've got to do what you've got to do. And the other,
平清盛，也就是那个一直在濑户内海活动、建造所有那些港口、疏浚航道等等的人。他处死了他的叔叔。我认为这很公平。我的意思是，叔叔就是叔叔，但父亲是另一回事。这是京都三百年来的第一次公开处决。它们让所有人毫无疑问地意识到，一个新时代已经到来。因为这些被处决的权贵的头颅被插在长矛上，放在京都的市场上。所以每个人都能亲眼看到发生了什么。等等。日本距离上一次公开处决和这些杀戮之间的时间跨度……

37
00:26:31.000 --> 00:27:09.000
Tyrono Kiyomori, so he is the guy who's been operating in the inland sea, building all those ports and dredging the shipping lanes and all that kind of thing. He puts his uncle to death. I think that's fair enough. I mean, an uncle is an uncle, but your father is a different matter. These are the first public executions in Kyoto for three and a half centuries. And they leave no one in any doubt that a new era has dawned. Because the head. heads of these executed grandees are put on spikes and put up in the Kyoto marketplace. So everyone can see for themselves what's going on. So hold on. There's a bigger gap in Japan between the last public executions and these killings
比我们明天在英国进行公开处决与之前的公开处决之间的时间跨度还要大。所以这一定是一个巨大的文化冲击。是的，巨大的文化冲击。以防万一，你知道，首都里的任何人还没意识到一个新时代已经到来，他们需要为文化冲击做好准备，更多的戏剧性事件即将发生。因为三年后的1159年，源义朝，也就是那个据说砍下了他父亲头颅的人，非常愤恨他的平氏盟友清盛在朝廷获得的奖赏比他多。他想，去他的，我要发动政变。他与一位非常不满……

